2026-08-24T19:01:04Z	tried	what=the recovery demonstration: kill -9 mid-flight, wipe the box, continue from the repo	config=kill -9 on supervisor and worker (no run_stop, no final bank), rm -rf the checkout and the harness state on the box, then a clean clone + build + tmhaul recover + tmhaul watch --detach	produced=detected from the repo alone as CRITICAL zero_throughput 12m after the last sample; recovered with resume_evals=80 read out of the repo; throughput resumed 80 -> 160 -> 189 evals and every alarm cleared. Cost of the crash: 34 evals, about 90 seconds. Clone 3.8 s, build 4.9 s.	claim=MEASURED	control=the same fresh checkout, 1m34s after the kill, fired NOTHING — so the alarm is capable of silence, which is the half that makes the firing mean something. And the mirror's 17 files came back byte-identical to the clone's: two transports (GitHub via a deploy key on Windows, a paste via x509) with nothing in common, agreeing.	why=This is the deliverable that proves the rest. Durability claims are cheap to write and this project has been burned by exactly that; the only convincing form is to destroy the box and show the work continue. It also produced the honest bound: a crash costs at most one banking window of RECORD, and artifacts survive only if the worker writes them into state/frontier -- which the rung-1 explorer will have to do and the sweep did not need to.	node=117796
